#!/bin/sh /etc/rc.common
# Pin the RM520N's PCIe endpoint in D0.
#
# mhi_pci_generic calls pm_runtime_allow() once the modem reaches mission
# mode (MHI_CB_EE_MISSION_MODE) and sets a 2s autosuspend delay, so the
# endpoint cycles D0<->D3hot continuously — measured at ~79% of wall time
# in D3. vjt's README documents that "PCIe runtime PM kills MHI ... the
# modem firmware crashes (CmpltTO + AER cascade) when the PCIe link tries
# to enter D3hot", and mitigates it with pcie_port_pm=off in bootargs —
# but that governs the PCIe PORT service driver, not the endpoint's own
# runtime PM, so the endpoint keeps suspending regardless (confirmed: the
# bootarg is live in /proc/cmdline and the cycling still happens).
#
# Two reasons to pin it: it stops the D3hot transitions vjt identifies as
# fatal to MHI, and each cycle emits ~19 lines of pci_dbg() config-space
# spam that overruns the 128K kernel log buffer (CONFIG_LOG_BUF_SHIFT=17)
# in minutes — which destroys both dmesg history and the ramoops panic
# record we rely on for crash diagnosis.
#
# Costs a little idle power. Revert at runtime with:
#   echo auto > /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/power/control
START=94
start() {
	D=/sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:01:00.0/power/control
	[ -e "$D" ] && echo on > "$D"
}
